-- title:
--   Mumford truncation computes coker(d⊗ A) for all A
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $d\colon C^0\to C^1$ be an $R$-linear map of $R$-modules (all types in one universe) such that the quotient $C^1/\operatorname{range} d$ is a finite $R$-module. Attached to $d$ are: a natural number $\operatorname{rank} d$ chosen so that $C^1/\operatorname{range} d$ is generated by that many elements, together with a chosen surjection from the free module $R^{\operatorname{rank} d}=(\mathrm{Fin}(\operatorname{rank} d)\to R)$ onto it; a map $\mathrm{lift}\,d\colon R^{\operatorname{rank} d}\to C^1$ lifting that surjection through the quotient map $C^1\to C^1/\operatorname{range} d$, obtained from projectivity of the free module; the submodule $K^0\subseteq C^0\times R^{\operatorname{rank} d}$ that is the kernel of the coproduct map $(x,t)\mapsto d(x)+(\mathrm{lift}\,d)(t)$, with $\iota_0 d$ and $\mathrm{dK}\,d$ the restrictions to $K^0$ of the two projections and $\iota_1 d=-\,\mathrm{lift}\,d$; and the identity $\mathrm{comm}\,d\colon d\circ\iota_0 d=\iota_1 d\circ \mathrm{dK}\,d$. The assertion is that for every commutative $R$-algebra $A$ the $A$-linear map
--   $$\bigl(A\otimes_R R^{\operatorname{rank} d}\bigr)/\operatorname{range}\bigl(\mathrm{dK}\,d\otimes A\bigr)\longrightarrow \bigl(A\otimes_R C^1\bigr)/\operatorname{range}\bigl(d\otimes A\bigr)$$
--   induced on quotients by $\iota_1 d\otimes A$ (well defined by $\mathrm{comm}\,d$) is bijective. No flatness hypothesis on $A$ is imposed.
--
--   This is the degree-one half of the statement that Mumford's truncation of a two-term complex computes its cohomology compatibly with arbitrary base change, as in Mumford's base-change lemma for Abelian varieties and Hartshorne III.12.2; at $A=R$ it says $R^{\operatorname{rank} d}/\operatorname{range}(\mathrm{dK}\,d)\cong C^1/\operatorname{range} d$. It is used in the two-chart Čech development to obtain projectivity of the kernel term when the fibrewise map is surjective, and thence local constancy of the fibrewise Euler characteristic.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_TwoChartCech_Mumford_bijective_cokerMapBaseChange.lean

import Definitions.Def_AlgebraicGeometry_MumfordTruncation
